#!zsh
# auto-fu: inline auto-completion for zsh
#
# As you type, auto-fu runs the completion system and shows the unambiguous
# part of the match inline - highlighted, and *not* part of the buffer.  Tab
# commits it and starts cycling; any other key just keeps typing over it.
#
# Fork of auto-fu.zsh by Takeshi Banse, split into parts that are sourced in
# dependency order:
#
#   auto-fu-predicates  "may we complete here?" - one call per keystroke
#   auto-fu-highlight   $region_highlight bookkeeping + zsh-syntax-highlighting
#   auto-fu-complete    completion-system glue and the Tab handler
#   auto-fu-core        the engine: state, keymap tracking, line init
#   auto-fu-widgets     the afu+* ZLE widgets
#   auto-fu-install     widget registration, keymaps, ZLE rebinding
#
# Entry point: auto-fu-install.  The prezto completion module calls it and sets
# every ':auto-fu:*' zstyle; see modules/completion/init.zsh.
#
# The central invariant: the inline completion is never part of the real
# buffer.  $buffer_cur holds the line as the user actually typed it, and
# $afu_in_p is 1 while a completion is displayed but not committed.  Anything
# that accepts, runs, or hands off the line must restore $buffer_cur first, or
# the user runs a command they only half typed.  modules/fzf/init.zsh relies on
# both of those names.
#
# Keystroke path, for orientation:
#
#   self-insert
#     -> afu+self-insert               (auto-fu-widgets)
#     -> with-afu~                     zle auto-fu-extend --
#     -> with-afu-magic-insert         undo an inserted char if needed
#     -> with-afu-resume
#     -> with-afu-zsh-syntax-highlighting
#     -> with-afu                      run .self-insert, then maybe complete
#     -> auto-fu-maybe -> auto-fu      the engine

typeset -g _afu_dir="${0:A:h}"

for _afu_part in predicates highlight complete core widgets install; do
	source "$_afu_dir/auto-fu-$_afu_part"
done
unset _afu_part
